<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <application
        android:name=".BrainingApp"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Braining"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config">

        <!--
            `tools:ignore="Instantiatable"` — a lint FALSE POSITIVE, silenced here and nowhere
            wider. Added 2026-08-30, on the first `assembleRelease` this project has ever run.

            **What lint claims:** "MainActivity must extend android.app.Activity".
            **What is true:** `MainActivity : AppCompatActivity()`, and `AppCompatActivity`
            extends `FragmentActivity` → `ComponentActivity` → `android.app.Activity`. The class
            is public, has the implicit no-arg constructor, and is not an inner class.

            **The evidence that settles it is not an argument, it is a fact:** the debug APK has
            been installed and opened on the owner's phone dozens of times. Android instantiated
            this activity every one of them. A class the platform has actually constructed cannot
            be uninstantiatable.

            **Why lint gets it wrong.** Two candidates and it is not worth another build to
            separate them: `@AndroidEntryPoint` makes Hilt rewrite the superclass to a generated
            `Hilt_MainActivity` in bytecode, which lint may not have in scope — or lint cannot
            resolve `AppCompatActivity`'s hierarchy out of the AAR. Note that `.BrainingApp` on
            the `<application>` tag did NOT trip the same check, and it extends a *framework*
            class directly rather than a library one. That asymmetry points at resolution, not at
            the code.

            **Why it appears only now:** `lintVitalRelease` runs on release builds alone, and
            until 2026-08-30 this project had only ever built debug. The false positive has
            presumably been true for months.

            **Why `tools:ignore` and not `lint { disable += "Instantiatable" }`:** this silences
            exactly one declaration. Every other component — the application class, and any
            service or receiver added later — is still checked, and a real uninstantiatable class
            will still fail the release build. Disabling the rule module-wide would trade one
            false positive for a permanently blind check.

            **If this is ever revisited:** delete the attribute and run `assembleRelease`. If
            lint has learned to resolve the hierarchy, it passes and this comment goes with it.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Braining"
            tools:ignore="Instantiatable">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!--
            M6 — hands an exported Markdown file to the share sheet as a `content://` URI.

            `exported="false"` with `grantUriPermissions="true"` is the pair that matters: no other
            app may query this provider, and the receiving app gets read access to exactly the one
            URI it was handed, for as long as that one Intent lives. `file_paths.xml` limits it to
            `cache/exports/`.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>
    </application>
</manifest>
